module Tool.Hex exposing (Model, Msg, command, init, menu, menuItem, openFile, receive, update, view, windowId)

{-| The hex dump viewer: offsets, sixteen bytes a row, and the printable
rendering beside them.

**It never holds the file.** A window shows sixteen rows of sixteen bytes --
two hundred and fifty-six of them -- and `FileSystem.readFileStream` takes a
`Between { start, end }`, which is Node's `createReadStream({start, end})` and
reads exactly the range asked for. So the model keeps the file's *size*, from
`FileSystem.metadata`, and one 16 KB chunk around wherever the view is. A file
of any size opens at once; a file under 16 KB is a single read and behaves as
if it had been loaded whole.

That is the answer to a question this tool could not avoid -- what happens when
somebody opens a four gigabyte core dump -- and it is a better answer than the
obvious one. The obvious one is `metadata` for the size and a refusal above
some number: it works, it costs a dialog explaining a limit, and it puts a
number in the program that nobody can defend. Reading only the window removes
the question instead of answering it.

Three things follow, and they are the interesting part:

**The chunk is a cache, so a read that arrives late must be ignored.** Scrolling
asks for bytes; scrolling again before they land asks for others. Every answer
carries the range it was asked for, and one that is not the range still wanted
is dropped -- without which a fast `PgDn PgDn` can leave the older answer on
screen.

**A tool that does I/O hands a `Cmd` back to the shell**, which no tool did
before this one: `Main.toTools` mapped over the tools and threw commands away,
because a chart and a calculator never had one. It needs a `Msg` type of its
own as well, and therefore a `Tui.Ports` record at that type -- which is one
extra binding in `Main` and nothing more, because port functions are
polymorphic.

**Sixteen bytes a row is a choice, and the window is told to enforce it.**
It did not start that way. `Resized` reports the *desktop*, never a window, so
nothing told a model that the user had widened this one -- and a canvas's lines
are the model's, so a dump that reflowed to its window needed a fact the
program could not have. This is the tool that made the package grow the fact:
`Tui.WindowResized` says how big a window is now, and `Tui.Resize` on the
window says which of its dimensions the user may change at all.

Which turns the width into an argument rather than a limitation, and the
argument comes out the other way. Sixteen is what a hex dump is: it makes the
low nibble of the offset the column number, so the byte at `0x4C` is in column
C of the row starting `40`, and that is the whole reason a dump is laid out in
a grid. A twenty-three byte row would be a table nobody can read an offset off.
So the window says `resize = Tui.resizeHeight`, the frame stops offering a
width the model would refuse to use, and sixteen stays forced -- by the format
now, rather than by the API.

Height is the opposite: more rows is more file, and the tool wants all it can
get. The canvas grows by itself, because [`Grows`](Tui#Grow) is what
`TGroup::changeBounds` is for, and `WindowResized` is what tells the model to
put more lines in it. Both halves are needed and the failure modes are
different: without the wrapper the canvas stays sixteen rows in a taller
window; without the event it is a taller canvas with sixteen rows of dump and
blank space under them.

**Highlighting is vim's visual mode, and that is not a decoration.** `v` marks
from the cursor, `V` marks whole rows, every arrow and every click moves the
far end, and one of `1`-`6` paints what is marked. Nothing else in the program
needed a keyboard gesture with a *state* in it, and the alternatives were both
worse: `Shift`-arrows extend without a mode but say nothing about what a mouse
click means while they are held, and `Ctrl-Shift-V` -- the shape that would
have mimicked vim's third mode -- is the paste binding of every terminal
emulator on this machine and never arrives. `v` and `V` are plain letters,
which no terminal intercepts and no modifier report is needed for, and the
canvas has focus so nothing else wants them.

The mouse extends the mark for free, and that is worth saying because it is not
a feature that was written: a click already goes through
[`moveTo`](#moveTo), the mark's far end *is* the cursor, so a click while
marking moves it. What is not free is dragging -- the binding forwards
`evMouseDown` and nothing else, so there is no press-move-release to hear.

**And there are two of it now.** `p` and `P` fill the window from the
clipboard instead of from a file -- as the text's own bytes, or as the bytes
its hex digits spell out. That is a second *source*, and the surprise was how
little of this file it reached: a pasted buffer is a source whose every byte is
already in the chunk, so `covers` says yes to every range, `load` never reads,
and the dump, the scrolling, the marking, the highlighting and the copying were
all already written against a size and a chunk. The union is
[`Origin`](#Origin) and it has exactly one job, which is answering "where do
more bytes come from".

-}

import Array exposing (Array)
import Array.Builder as Builder
import Ascii
import Bytes exposing (Bytes)
import Bytes.Decode as Decode
import Char
import FileSystem
import FileSystem.Path as Path exposing (Path)
import Json.Decode
import Math
import Stream
import String
import Task exposing (Task)
import Theme exposing (Inks)
import Tui exposing (Hue(..), MenuItem(..), Span, View(..))


-- NAMES


command : String
command =
    "tool.hex"


windowId : String
windowId =
    "hex"


canvasId : String
canvasId =
    "hex.dump"


scrollId : String
scrollId =
    "hex.scroll"


{-| The file dialog's id and the goto dialog's, deliberately not the same
strings as the commands that open them. A `DialogClosed` is keyed by the
dialog and a `Command` by the menu entry, and `update` is easier to read when
the two cannot be mistaken for each other.
-}
fileDialogId : String
fileDialogId =
    "hex.file"


gotoDialogId : String
gotoDialogId =
    "hex.goto.dialog"


gotoFieldId : String
gotoFieldId =
    "hex.goto.offset"


menuItem : MenuItem
menuItem =
    Item { title = "Hex ~d~ump viewer", cmd = command, key = "Alt-D", shortcut = "Alt-D" }


-- SHAPE


{-| Sixteen, and forced -- see the note at the top of the file.
-}
bytesPerRow : Int
bytesPerRow =
    16


{-| How many dump rows the window shows when it opens. Not a minimum: the
window is [`Tui.resizeHeight`](Tui#resizeHeight), so the user can drag it
shorter as well as taller.
-}
openRows : Int
openRows =
    16


{-| How many rows of a window of this height are the dump.

Six are spoken for whatever the height is: two frame rows, the column header,
the blank row under the dump, and the two lines describing the byte under the
cursor. Written once because it is used twice -- to lay the window out, and to
turn a [`Tui.WindowResized`](Tui#Event) event into a row count -- and those two
agreeing is the whole of the arithmetic.

Turbo Vision will not let a window go below six rows, which is one row short of
having a dump at all, so the floor here is one rather than zero: `page` is a
divisor and a screenful of nothing is not a number to do arithmetic with.
-}
rowsIn : Int -> Int
rowsIn height =
    max 1 (height - 6)


{-| One screenful: what `PgUp` moves by, and how much has to be in memory for
the window to draw.
-}
page : Model -> Int
page model =
    model.rows * bytesPerRow


{-| How much is read at a time. Sixty-four screenfuls, so scrolling crosses a
chunk boundary about once every five hundred rows, and small enough that
decoding it into an `Array Int` is not something anybody notices.
-}
chunkSize : Int
chunkSize =
    16384


{-| Where the columns are, in the canvas's own coordinates. Each of these is
used twice -- once to draw and once to work out which byte a click landed on --
which is why they are named rather than written into both.

    00000010  48 65 6C 6C 6F 20 77 6F  72 6C 64 21 0A 00 01 02  Hello world!····

-}
hexStart : Int
hexStart =
    10


asciiStart : Int
asciiStart =
    60


lineWidth : Int
lineWidth =
    76


-- MODEL


type alias Model =
    { permission : FileSystem.Permission
    -- Dialogs centre themselves, so a tool that opens one needs the desktop's
    -- size the same way the shell does. It arrives as an event, and every open
    -- tool is handed every event, so this costs nothing but the field.
    , desktop : { cols : Int, rows : Int }
    , open : Maybe Source
    -- The offset of the first byte on screen, always a multiple of sixteen.
    , top : Int
    , cursor : Int
    , chunk : Chunk
    -- The read in flight, by the range it asked for -- which is also the
    -- guard: an answer that is not this range answers a question no longer
    -- being asked.
    , wanted : Maybe Range
    -- How many dump rows the window is showing. Sixteen until the user drags
    -- the window taller, and then whatever fits. The canvas grows by itself --
    -- `Grows` is what does that -- but its *lines* are the model's, so a taller
    -- canvas the model was not told about would be sixteen rows of dump with
    -- blank space under them.
    , rows : Int
    , browsing : Maybe { path : Path, entries : Array Entry }
    , recent : Array String
    -- What the user has painted, oldest first, and the range being dragged
    -- out. Both are offsets into *this* file, so opening another one drops
    -- them; closing the window drops the whole model, which is the rule every
    -- tool here follows.
    , highlights : Array Highlight
    , marking : Maybe Marking
    -- What the last yank sent, worded for the message line. The answer to a
    -- copy is an event and not a return value, so the sentence has to wait
    -- somewhere for it: `Copied` says whether the system clipboard took the
    -- text and nothing about what the text *was*.
    , yanked : Maybe String
    -- A paste asked for and not yet answered, and how the answer is to be
    -- read. The clipboard replies with an event and every open tool is handed
    -- every event, so without this a paste in another tool would land here.
    , awaiting : Maybe Reading
    , message : String
    }


{-| A range of the file the user has painted, and which of the six inks they
painted it with. Half open, like every range in this file.

They are kept in the order they were made and the *last* one wins wherever two
overlap, which is the whole of the overlap policy. The alternative -- splitting
the older range around the newer as it is painted -- needs an answer to what
happens when the newer one is taken off again, and there is no answer to that
a person would predict. Paint over something and then take the paint off is
one they would.
-}
type alias Highlight =
    { start : Int, end : Int, ink : Int }


{-| The mark being dragged out: where it started, and whether it counts in
bytes or in rows. The other end is not stored, because the other end is the
*cursor* -- so every arrow key, every page, every click and every jump to an
offset extends the mark without any of them knowing that it does.
-}
type alias Marking =
    { anchor : Int, grain : Grain }


type Grain
    = ByByte
    | ByRow


{-| The two things a range of a file is worth pasting somewhere as.

`AsHex` is the bytes and nothing else -- `48 65 6C 6C 6F` -- which is what goes
into a debugger, a test fixture or an argument to something. `AsDump` is what is
on the screen, offsets and printable column included, which is what goes into a
bug report. Neither is recoverable from the other by whatever receives it, which
is why there are two.
-}
type Shape
    = AsHex
    | AsDump


{-| What the text on the clipboard is taken to *be*.

Two commands rather than one that decides for itself, and the reason is that
deciding is not possible. `beef`, `cafe`, `decade` and `0123456789` are all
words somebody might paste and all valid hex, so a program that sniffs is a
program that is sometimes silently wrong about what it is showing -- and the
whole point of a hex dump is that you are looking at it to find out what is
really there.
-}
type Reading
    = Characters
    | HexDigits


{-| What is on the screen: how big it is, what to call it, and where more of
it comes from.

The third of those is the only thing that differs between the two, and it is
worth being a union rather than a `Maybe Path`. A file is read a chunk at a
time and a paste is *all here* -- so the question "can I have the bytes at
offset n" has a different answer for each, and every place that asks has to
say which it is dealing with. A `Maybe Path` would let a missing path mean
"do nothing", which is what a bug looks like from the inside.

Everything else about them is the same, and that is the useful half: the model
holds a size and one chunk of bytes either way, so the dump, the scrolling, the
marking and the copying were all written once and did not notice this arriving.
-}
type alias Source =
    { name : String, size : Int, from : Origin }


type Origin
    = OnDisk Path
    | Pasted


type alias Chunk =
    { start : Int, bytes : Array Int }


{-| Half open: `start` up to but not including `end`, which is how every range
in this file is written and not how `readFileStream` takes one. The conversion
happens in exactly one place, in [`read`](#read).
-}
type alias Range =
    { start : Int, end : Int }


type alias Entry =
    { name : String, path : Path, isDir : Bool }


type Msg
    = Listed { path : Path, entries : Array { path : Path, entityType : FileSystem.EntityType } }
    | Statted { path : Path, meta : FileSystem.Metadata }
    | ReadBack { range : Range, bytes : Bytes }
    | Failed String


type alias Step =
    { model : Model, command : Cmd Msg }


init : FileSystem.Permission -> Model
init permission =
    { permission = permission
    , desktop = { cols = 80, rows = 23 }
    , open = Nothing
    , top = 0
    , cursor = 0
    , chunk = { start = 0, bytes = [] }
    , wanted = Nothing
    , rows = openRows
    , browsing = Nothing
    , recent = []
    , highlights = []
    , marking = Nothing
    , yanked = Nothing
    , awaiting = Nothing
    , message = ""
    }


{-| The same viewer, already pointed at a file: `predc hex dump.bin`.

It is `statFile` and not a read, which is the same path the file dialog takes
and for the same reason -- whether a name is a file or a directory is a
question about the file system, and the answer decides between loading it and
listing it. A name that is neither answers `Failed`, so the window opens with
the reason on its message line rather than the program refusing to start over
it. The dump is the place a person is looking; the complaint belongs there.
-}
openFile : FileSystem.Permission -> Path -> Step
openFile permission path =
    let
        model =
            init permission
    in
    { model = model, command = statFile model path }


still : Model -> Step
still model =
    { model = model, command = Cmd.none }


-- UPDATE


{-| The shell hands over every event it was given; a tool answers for its own
ids and ignores the rest.

`ports` is here because two of these branches open a dialog, and `Tui.dialog`
takes the port record at the type of the message it will eventually produce.
The shell's is at the shell's `Msg`; this one is at ours, and `Main` builds it
from the same two port functions.
-}
update : Tui.Ports Msg -> Tui.Event -> Model -> Step
update ports event model =
    when event is
        Tui.Resized size ->
            still { model | desktop = size }

        -- The user dragged the window taller or shorter. Only the height can
        -- change -- `resize = Tui.resizeHeight` in the view below -- so the
        -- only thing to work out is how many rows of dump now fit, and the
        -- only thing to do about it is read enough bytes to fill them.
        --
        -- The window's *rectangle* is deliberately not stored. What the model
        -- renders is what the differ compares against, so a rectangle it never
        -- changes is a `setBounds` never written and a window left exactly
        -- where the user put it.
        Tui.WindowResized it ->
            if it.id == windowId then
                let
                    grown =
                        { model | rows = rowsIn (it.rect.y2 - it.rect.y1) }
                in
                -- Through `scrollTo` rather than straight to `load`, because
                -- a window that just got shorter can leave `top` past the last
                -- row there is anything to show above.
                scrollTo grown.top grown

            else
                still model

        Tui.KeyPressed pressed ->
            if pressed.id == canvasId then
                pressKey ports pressed.key model

            else
                still model

        Tui.Clicked spot ->
            if spot.id == canvasId then
                clickAt spot.x spot.y model

            else
                still model

        -- Dragging across the dump marks it, and almost none of that is
        -- written here. The press moved the cursor and said nothing else; the
        -- first cell the pointer crosses is what turns that into a mark,
        -- anchored where the press landed. Everything after it is the ordinary
        -- `clickAt`, because the far end of a mark *is* the cursor -- the same
        -- reason `v` and the arrow keys needed no code between them.
        --
        -- So the mouse draws the same mark `v` does, `1`-`6` paint what it
        -- drew, and the legend under the dump appears while it is being drawn
        -- without this knowing that a legend exists.
        Tui.Dragged spot ->
            if spot.id == canvasId then
                dragTo spot.x spot.y model

            else
                still model

        -- A drag on the *bar* reports only where it ended, so this is one move
        -- and not a dozen. The cursor comes along with the view rather than
        -- being left behind: it is what the two lines under the dump describe, and a
        -- description of a byte nobody can see is worse than none.
        Tui.Scrolled bar ->
            if bar.id == scrollId then
                scrollTo (bar.value * bytesPerRow) model

            else
                still model

        Tui.Command "hex.open" ->
            { model = { model | message = "" }, command = browseFrom model }

        Tui.Command "hex.goto" ->
            when model.open is
                Nothing ->
                    still { model | message = "Nothing is open." }

                Just _ ->
                    { model = model, command = Tui.dialog ports (gotoDialog model) }

        -- Every open tool is handed every event, so the guard is not
        -- politeness: `yanked` is what says this tool is the one that asked.
        Tui.Copied it ->
            when model.yanked is
                Just what ->
                    still
                        { model
                            | yanked = Nothing
                            , message =
                                "Copied "
                                    ++ what
                                    ++ (if it.toSystem then
                                            "."

                                        else
                                            -- Not "nothing took it", which is
                                            -- what this used to say and was
                                            -- wrong: the `OSC 52` goes out
                                            -- every time and the terminal
                                            -- simply never answers for it.
                                            -- See the README, and `Tui.Copied`.
                                            " -- the terminal did not confirm it."
                                       )
                        }

                Nothing ->
                    still model

        -- Same guard as `Copied`, and it earns itself here: `awaiting` is
        -- what says this tool asked, and it carries what to do with the
        -- answer, which nothing in the event says.
        Tui.ClipboardText it ->
            when model.awaiting is
                Just reading ->
                    paste reading it.text { model | awaiting = Nothing }

                Nothing ->
                    still model

        Tui.Command "hex.pasteText" ->
            askClipboard ports Characters model

        Tui.Command "hex.pasteHex" ->
            askClipboard ports HexDigits model

        Tui.Command "hex.copyHex" ->
            yank ports AsHex model

        Tui.Command "hex.copyDump" ->
            yank ports AsDump model

        Tui.Command "hex.mark" ->
            mark ByByte model

        Tui.Command "hex.markRows" ->
            mark ByRow model

        Tui.Command "hex.unpaint" ->
            unpaint model

        Tui.Command "hex.clear" ->
            if Array.isEmpty model.highlights then
                still { model | message = "Nothing is highlighted." }

            else
                still { model | highlights = [], marking = Nothing, message = "" }

        Tui.Command "hex.top" ->
            moveTo 0 model

        Tui.Command "hex.end" ->
            when model.open is
                Nothing ->
                    still model

                Just source ->
                    moveTo (source.size - 1) model

        Tui.DialogClosed closed ->
            if closed.id == fileDialogId then
                answerFile closed model

            else if closed.id == gotoDialogId then
                answerGoto closed model

            else
                still model

        -- The six colours are one menu entry each and one key each, so they
        -- are one command each with the ink's number on the end rather than
        -- six branches saying the same thing.
        Tui.Command cmd ->
            if String.startsWith inkCommand cmd then
                when inkOf (String.dropFirst (String.count inkCommand) cmd) is
                    Just index ->
                        paintWith index model

                    Nothing ->
                        still model

            else
                still model

        _ ->
            still model


{-| The tool's own messages: what the three tasks it starts answer with.
-}
receive : Tui.Ports Msg -> Msg -> Model -> Step
receive ports msg model =
    when msg is
        Listed listed ->
            let
                browsing =
                    { path = listed.path, entries = entriesOf listed }
            in
            { model = { model | browsing = Just browsing }
            , command = Tui.dialog ports (fileDialog model browsing)
            }

        -- Which of the two a name is, is a question about the file system and
        -- not about the name, so it is answered here rather than by reading
        -- what the dialog said. A directory reopens the dialog one level down,
        -- which is what the user meant by choosing it.
        Statted it ->
            when it.meta.entityType is
                FileSystem.Directory ->
                    { model = model, command = browse model it.path }

                FileSystem.File ->
                    load
                        { model
                            | open =
                                Just
                                    { name = Path.filenameWithExtension it.path
                                    , size = it.meta.byteSize
                                    , from = OnDisk it.path
                                    }
                            , top = 0
                            , cursor = 0
                            , chunk = { start = 0, bytes = [] }
                            , wanted = Nothing
                            -- An offset means nothing in a different file.
                            , highlights = []
                            , marking = Nothing
                            , yanked = Nothing
                            , awaiting = Nothing
                            , browsing = Nothing
                            , recent = remember (Path.toPosixString it.path) model.recent
                            , message =
                                if it.meta.byteSize == 0 then
                                    "The file is empty."

                                else
                                    ""
                        }

                _ ->
                    still
                        { model
                            | message = "Not a regular file: " ++ Path.toPosixString it.path
                            , browsing = Nothing
                        }

        ReadBack it ->
            if model.wanted == Just it.range then
                load
                    { model
                        | chunk = { start = it.range.start, bytes = toByteArray it.bytes }
                        , wanted = Nothing
                    }

            else
                still model

        Failed reason ->
            still { model | message = reason }


-- KEYS


pressKey : Tui.Ports Msg -> String -> Model -> Step
pressKey ports key model =
    when key is
        -- The two keys that need nothing open, because they are one of the
        -- two ways of opening something.
        "p" ->
            askClipboard ports Characters model

        "P" ->
            askClipboard ports HexDigits model

        _ ->
            whenOpen ports key model


whenOpen : Tui.Ports Msg -> String -> Model -> Step
whenOpen ports key model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just source ->
            when key is
                "Left" ->
                    moveTo (model.cursor - 1) model

                "Right" ->
                    moveTo (model.cursor + 1) model

                "Up" ->
                    moveTo (model.cursor - bytesPerRow) model

                "Down" ->
                    moveTo (model.cursor + bytesPerRow) model

                "PgUp" ->
                    pageBy -1 model

                "PgDn" ->
                    pageBy 1 model

                "Home" ->
                    moveTo (rowStart model.cursor) model

                "End" ->
                    moveTo (rowStart model.cursor + bytesPerRow - 1) model

                "Ctrl-Home" ->
                    moveTo 0 model

                "Ctrl-End" ->
                    moveTo (source.size - 1) model

                -- vim's two visual modes, and vim's answer to pressing the
                -- same one twice.
                "v" ->
                    mark ByByte model

                "V" ->
                    mark ByRow model

                "Esc" ->
                    when model.marking is
                        Just _ ->
                            still { model | marking = Nothing, message = "" }

                        Nothing ->
                            still model

                "d" ->
                    unpaint model

                "y" ->
                    yank ports AsHex model

                other ->
                    when inkOf other is
                        Just index ->
                            paintWith index model

                        Nothing ->
                            still model


-- HIGHLIGHTING


{-| The prefix the six menu entries share. One command each rather than one
command with an argument, because a `MenuItem`'s `cmd` is a string and this is
what a string with a number on the end is for.

The number on the end is the one on the *key cap* -- `hex.ink3` is what `3`
does -- so that both ends of this go through [`inkOf`](#inkOf) and there is
only one place that knows the six are numbered from one and indexed from zero.
The first version of this put the index on the end instead, and the menu
painted every range one colour to the left of the one it named.
-}
inkCommand : String
inkCommand =
    "hex.ink"


{-| `"3"` is the third ink, which is index two. Anything else -- a letter, a
seventh colour -- is not an ink at all, which is what makes this safe to run
over every key the canvas is sent.
-}
inkOf : String -> Maybe Int
inkOf text =
    when String.toInt text is
        Just number ->
            if number >= 1 && number <= Array.length Theme.highlightNames then
                Just (number - 1)

            else
                Nothing

        Nothing ->
            Nothing


{-| Start a mark, change its grain, or -- if it is already this grain -- stop
it. The same key that starts it stops it, which is the only way out of a mode
somebody finds without being told about it; `v` then `V` keeps the anchor and
widens to rows, which is what vim does with the same two keys.
-}
mark : Grain -> Model -> Step
mark grain model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still { model | message = "Nothing is open." }

        Just _ ->
            when model.marking is
                Just marking ->
                    if marking.grain == grain then
                        still { model | marking = Nothing, message = "" }

                    else
                        still { model | marking = Just { marking | grain = grain } }

                Nothing ->
                    still
                        { model
                            | marking = Just { anchor = model.cursor, grain = grain }
                            , message = ""
                        }


{-| What is marked right now: the anchor and the cursor, in whichever order the
user put them, snapped out to whole rows if the mark is a row mark.

Clamped to the end of the file, because the count in the status line is a
number the user will read and `V` on the last row of a file would otherwise
claim sixteen bytes that are not there.
-}
markedRange : Model -> Maybe Range
markedRange model =
    when model.marking is
        Nothing ->
            Nothing

        Just marking ->
            let
                low =
                    min marking.anchor model.cursor

                high =
                    max marking.anchor model.cursor

                raw =
                    when marking.grain is
                        ByByte ->
                            { start = low, end = high + 1 }

                        ByRow ->
                            { start = rowStart low, end = rowStart high + bytesPerRow }

                size =
                    when model.open is
                        Just source ->
                            source.size

                        Nothing ->
                            0
            in
            Just { start = raw.start, end = min size raw.end }


paintWith : Int -> Model -> Step
paintWith index model =
    when markedRange model is
        Nothing ->
            still { model | message = "Nothing is marked -- press v, move, then a number." }

        Just range ->
            still
                { model
                    | highlights =
                        model.highlights
                            ++ [ { start = range.start, end = range.end, ink = index } ]
                    , marking = Nothing
                    , message = ""
                }


unpaint : Model -> Step
unpaint model =
    when topmostAt model model.cursor is
        Nothing ->
            still { model | message = "No highlight under the cursor." }

        Just index ->
            still { model | highlights = Array.remove index model.highlights, message = "" }


{-| Which highlight the byte at this offset belongs to, by *index* -- the last
one that covers it, since the last one painted is the one on top. The index
rather than the highlight itself, because both callers want something else:
one wants its ink and the other wants to take it out of the array.
-}
topmostAt : Model -> Int -> Maybe Int
topmostAt model offset =
    Array.indexedFoldl
        (\index highlight found ->
            if offset >= highlight.start && offset < highlight.end then
                Just index

            else
                found
        )
        Nothing
        model.highlights


inkAt : Model -> Int -> Maybe Int
inkAt model offset =
    topmostAt model offset
        |> Maybe.andThen (\index -> Array.get index model.highlights)
        |> Maybe.map (\highlight -> highlight.ink)


within : Maybe Range -> Int -> Bool
within range offset =
    when range is
        Just it ->
            offset >= it.start && offset < it.end

        Nothing ->
            False


-- PASTING


{-| Ask for the clipboard, and remember what the answer is for.

The message is not decoration. `readClipboard` is a request, and on a terminal
that owns the clipboard the reply comes back through the input stream and may
take a moment or never come at all -- so the one state this program can be in
that it cannot draw is "waiting for an answer". Saying so costs a line that the
answer clears, and leaves the right thing on the screen in the case where
nothing arrives.
-}
askClipboard : Tui.Ports Msg -> Reading -> Model -> Step
askClipboard ports reading model =
    { model = { model | awaiting = Just reading, message = "Asking for the clipboard..." }
    , command = Tui.readClipboard ports
    }


paste : Reading -> String -> Model -> Step
paste reading text model =
    if text == "" then
        still { model | message = "There is nothing on the clipboard." }

    else
        when reading is
            Characters ->
                showBytes (toByteArray (Bytes.fromString text)) "pasted text" model

            HexDigits ->
                when hexBytes text is
                    Err reason ->
                        still { model | message = reason }

                    Ok codes ->
                        if Array.isEmpty codes then
                            still { model | message = "There are no hex digits on the clipboard." }

                        else
                            showBytes codes "pasted hex" model


{-| A pasted buffer is a file whose every byte is already in the chunk, which
is why so little of this program had to learn about it: `covers` says yes to
every range, so `load` never reads, and everything else was already written
against a size and a chunk.
-}
showBytes : Array Int -> String -> Model -> Step
showBytes codes name model =
    still
        { model
            | open = Just { name = name, size = Array.length codes, from = Pasted }
            , chunk = { start = 0, bytes = codes }
            , wanted = Nothing
            , top = 0
            , cursor = 0
            , highlights = []
            , marking = Nothing
            , yanked = Nothing
            , message = ""
        }


{-| Hex digits, in whatever the thing that produced them used between the
pairs: spaces, newlines, commas, colons, semicolons, hyphens, and `0x` in front
of each byte. `48 65 6C`, `48,65,6c`, `0x48 0x65` and `48656C` are all the same
three bytes.

**Anything else is refused, and named.** The temptation is to keep the hex and
drop the rest, which would let a whole `xxd` dump be pasted -- and would read
its offset column and its printable column as data, and produce a buffer that
is wrong in a way nobody can see. Refusing means the one thing this cannot
currently take is a dump with its offsets still on it, and the message says so
rather than leaving somebody to work it out from a screen of plausible
rubbish.
-}
hexBytes : String -> Result String (Array Int)
hexBytes text =
    let
        stripped =
            text
                |> String.replace "0x" ""
                |> String.replace "0X" ""

        strays =
            String.keepIf
                (\char -> not (Char.isHexDigit char || isSeparator char))
                stripped

        digits =
            String.keepIf Char.isHexDigit stripped
    in
    when String.popFirst strays is
        Just stray ->
            Err
                ("That is not hex: '"
                    ++ String.fromChar stray.first
                    ++ "' is not a hex digit. Offsets and a printable column have to come off first."
                )

        Nothing ->
            if Math.modBy 2 (String.count digits) /= 0 then
                Err
                    ("That is "
                        ++ String.fromInt (String.count digits)
                        ++ " hex digits, and a byte takes two."
                    )

            else
                Ok
                    (Array.mapAndKeepJust
                        (\index -> fromHex (String.slice (index * 2) (index * 2 + 2) digits))
                        (Array.range 0 (divideBy 2 (String.count digits) - 1))
                    )


isSeparator : Char -> Bool
isSeparator char =
    String.contains (String.fromChar char) " \t\n\r,;:-"


-- COPYING


{-| vim's key, and vim's meaning: take what is marked. What is different is
what it takes when *nothing* is marked -- the highlight under the cursor, if
there is one -- and that is what makes a highlight worth more than its colour.
Mark it once, paint it, and it is a range you can come back to and copy a day
later without marking it again.

The mark is dropped afterwards, as vim drops the selection, and the paint is
not: `y` is a read.
-}
yank : Tui.Ports Msg -> Shape -> Model -> Step
yank ports shape model =
    when yankRange model is
        Nothing ->
            still
                { model | message = "Nothing is marked, and the cursor is not in a highlight." }

        Just wanted ->
            let
                range =
                    shaped shape model wanted
            in
            when bytesIn model range is
                -- The file was never held, only a window of it, so a mark
                -- dragged across more of the file than the chunk covers is a
                -- range this program genuinely does not have. Saying so is
                -- better than copying the part it happens to be holding: a
                -- dump missing its middle looks exactly like a dump.
                Nothing ->
                    still
                        { model
                            | message =
                                "That is "
                                    ++ String.fromInt (range.end - range.start)
                                    ++ " bytes and only "
                                    ++ String.fromInt chunkSize
                                    ++ " are in memory at a time."
                        }

                Just codes ->
                    { model =
                        { model
                            | marking = Nothing
                            , yanked = Just (describeYank shape range)
                            , message = ""
                        }
                    , command = Tui.copyToClipboard ports (render shape range codes)
                    }


{-| What a yank would take: the mark, or failing that the highlight the cursor
is sitting in.
-}
yankRange : Model -> Maybe Range
yankRange model =
    when markedRange model is
        Just range ->
            Just range

        Nothing ->
            topmostAt model model.cursor
                |> Maybe.andThen (\index -> Array.get index model.highlights)
                |> Maybe.map (\highlight -> { start = highlight.start, end = highlight.end })


{-| A dump is rows, so copying one takes whole rows however the range was made
-- otherwise the first line of what lands in the bug report is indented by an
amount nothing explains.
-}
shaped : Shape -> Model -> Range -> Range
shaped shape model range =
    when shape is
        AsHex ->
            range

        AsDump ->
            let
                size =
                    when model.open is
                        Just source ->
                            source.size

                        Nothing ->
                            range.end
            in
            { start = rowStart range.start
            , end = min size (rowStart (max range.start (range.end - 1)) + bytesPerRow)
            }


{-| Every byte of the range, or nothing at all if any of them is not held.

Two passes and no `pushLast`: a Gren array is copied when it grows, so folding
sixteen thousand bytes into one an element at a time is quadratic and this is
the one place in the program with sixteen thousand of anything.
-}
bytesIn : Model -> Range -> Maybe (Array Int)
bytesIn model range =
    let
        offsets =
            Array.range range.start (range.end - 1)

        codes =
            Array.mapAndKeepJust (byteAt model) offsets
    in
    if Array.length codes == Array.length offsets && Array.length codes > 0 then
        Just codes

    else
        Nothing


render : Shape -> Range -> Array Int -> String
render shape range codes =
    when shape is
        AsHex ->
            String.join " " (Array.map hexPair codes)

        AsDump ->
            String.join "\n"
                (Array.indexedMap
                    (\index line -> dumpLine (range.start + index * bytesPerRow) line)
                    (rowsOf codes)
                )


hexPair : Int -> String
hexPair code =
    String.padLeft 2 '0' (Ascii.radix 16 code)


rowsOf : Array Int -> Array (Array Int)
rowsOf codes =
    Array.map
        (\index -> Array.slice (index * bytesPerRow) ((index + 1) * bytesPerRow) codes)
        (Array.range 0 (divideBy bytesPerRow (Array.length codes - 1)))


{-| One line of what the window shows, in text.

Not quite what the window shows: the printable column uses a full stop where
the screen uses a middle dot. The screen can afford a character that says
"nothing here" more clearly than a full stop; a clipboard cannot, because what
is on the other end of it may be ASCII only, and every hex dump ever printed
has used a full stop for this.
-}
dumpLine : Int -> Array Int -> String
dumpLine base codes =
    offsetText base
        ++ "  "
        -- The screen's own hex field, so a short last row still leaves the
        -- printable column where every other row put it.
        ++ String.padRight (asciiStart - hexStart - 2)
            ' '
            (String.join " " (Array.map hexPair (Array.takeFirst 8 codes))
                ++ "  "
                ++ String.join " " (Array.map hexPair (Array.dropFirst 8 codes))
            )
        ++ "  "
        ++ String.join ""
            (Array.map
                (\code ->
                    if code >= 32 && code < 127 then
                        Ascii.glyph code

                    else
                        "."
                )
                codes
            )


describeYank : Shape -> Range -> String
describeYank shape range =
    String.fromInt (range.end - range.start)
        ++ (if range.end - range.start == 1 then
                " byte"

            else
                " bytes"
           )
        ++ (when shape is
                AsHex ->
                    " as hex"

                AsDump ->
                    " as a dump"
           )


-- MOVING AROUND


{-| A click on the hex column and a click on the printable one pick the same
byte, which is the point of drawing them side by side. A click to the left of
both lands on the first byte of the row: the user pointed at a row, and the row
is an answer.
-}
clickAt : Int -> Int -> Model -> Step
clickAt x y model =
    let
        line =
            clamp 0 (model.rows - 1) y

        column =
            if x >= asciiStart then
                clamp 0 (bytesPerRow - 1) (x - asciiStart)

            else if x >= hexStart then
                clamp 0 (bytesPerRow - 1) (byteOfColumn (x - hexStart))

            else
                0
    in
    moveTo (model.top + line * bytesPerRow + column) model


{-| The same byte a click would pick, and a mark anchored where the cursor
already was if there was not one out already.

`clickAt` clamps, which is what makes a drag pulled off the window harmless:
[`Tui.Dragged`](Tui#Event) reports the canvas's own coordinates and does not
clamp them, so the row is `-1` above the dump and `rows` below it, and both end
up on the nearest row of the window.

What it deliberately does *not* do is scroll. A terminal reports motion when
the pointer moves and not while it is held still, so "keep scrolling while I
hold it off the bottom" needs a timer of its own; a drag that scrolled one row
per twitch would be worse than one that stops at the edge. Mark to the edge,
`PgDn`, and go on marking -- the mark is still out and the cursor is still its
far end.
-}
dragTo : Int -> Int -> Model -> Step
dragTo x y model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just _ ->
            clickAt x
                y
                (when model.marking is
                    Just _ ->
                        model

                    Nothing ->
                        { model
                            | marking = Just { anchor = model.cursor, grain = ByByte }
                            , message = ""
                        }
                )


{-| Undo [`hexColumn`](#hexColumn): three columns to a byte, and one more from
the gap that splits the row in two.
-}
byteOfColumn : Int -> Int
byteOfColumn dx =
    if dx >= 25 then
        8 + ((dx - 25) // 3)

    else
        dx // 3


{-| A screenful moves the view *and* the cursor, which is not what falling
back on `moveTo` would do: that scrolls the smallest amount that keeps the
cursor visible, so a `PgDn` would leave the cursor pinned to the bottom row and
the next one would scroll by a row. A page means the same byte stays under the
same line of the window.
-}
pageBy : Int -> Model -> Step
pageBy direction model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just source ->
            let
                top =
                    clamp 0 (maxTop model source) (model.top + direction * page model)
            in
            load
                { model
                    | top = top
                    , cursor =
                        clamp 0
                            (max 0 (source.size - 1))
                            (top + Math.modBy (page model) (model.cursor - model.top))
                }


moveTo : Int -> Model -> Step
moveTo offset model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just source ->
            let
                cursor =
                    clamp 0 (max 0 (source.size - 1)) offset
            in
            load { model | cursor = cursor, top = keepInView model source cursor model.top }


{-| The smallest scroll that puts the cursor back on screen, which is what
every editor does and the only behaviour that survives holding an arrow key
down.
-}
keepInView : Model -> Source -> Int -> Int -> Int
keepInView model source cursor top =
    if cursor < top then
        rowStart cursor

    else if cursor >= top + page model then
        min (maxTop model source) (rowStart cursor - page model + bytesPerRow)

    else
        top


scrollTo : Int -> Model -> Step
scrollTo offset model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just source ->
            let
                top =
                    clamp 0 (maxTop model source) (rowStart offset)

                cursor =
                    clamp top (min (max 0 (source.size - 1)) (top + page model - 1)) model.cursor
            in
            load { model | top = top, cursor = cursor }


{-| Somebody who asked for an offset wants to see what is around it, so the row
lands a third of the way down rather than at the top.
-}
goTo : Int -> Model -> Step
goTo offset model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just source ->
            let
                cursor =
                    clamp 0 (max 0 (source.size - 1)) offset
            in
            load
                { model
                    | cursor = cursor
                    , top = clamp 0 (maxTop model source) (rowStart cursor - 5 * bytesPerRow)
                }


rowStart : Int -> Int
rowStart offset =
    offset - Math.modBy bytesPerRow offset


{-| The last `top` there is: the one that puts the final row on the bottom line
of the window.
-}
maxTop : Model -> Source -> Int
maxTop model source =
    max 0 (rowStart (max 0 (source.size - 1)) - page model + bytesPerRow)


{-| `//` truncates its *result* to 32 bits, which is fine for a column and
wrong for a file: byte 5,000,000,000 over sixteen is not a number an `Int32`
holds. Every offset in this file divides through here.
-}
divideBy : Int -> Int -> Int
divideBy divisor n =
    Math.truncate (toFloat n / toFloat divisor)


-- READING


{-| Make sure the bytes on screen are in memory, and ask for them if they are
not. Everything that moves the view ends here.
-}
load : Model -> Step
load model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just source ->
            let
                needed =
                    { start = model.top, end = min source.size (model.top + page model) }
            in
            if covers model.chunk needed then
                still model

            else
                let
                    range =
                        chunkAround source model.top
                in
                if model.wanted == Just range then
                    -- Already asked. Asking again would be one read of the
                    -- same bytes per keystroke while the first is in flight.
                    still model

                else
                    when source.from is
                        -- Unreachable rather than defensive: a pasted buffer
                        -- *is* its chunk, so `covers` above answered yes and
                        -- this branch is not on the way there. There is
                        -- nowhere to read from either.
                        Pasted ->
                            still model

                        OnDisk path ->
                            { model = { model | wanted = Just range }
                            , command = read model.permission path range
                            }


covers : Chunk -> Range -> Bool
covers chunk range =
    (range.end <= range.start)
        || (range.start >= chunk.start && range.end <= chunk.start + Array.length chunk.bytes)


{-| A chunk with the view in the middle of it, so that scrolling either way
from where the user is has the same room before the next read.
-}
chunkAround : Source -> Int -> Range
chunkAround source top =
    let
        start =
            clamp 0 (max 0 (source.size - chunkSize)) (rowStart (top - divideBy 2 chunkSize))
    in
    { start = start, end = min source.size (start + chunkSize) }


{-| The one place a half open range becomes Node's inclusive one.

`readFileStream` hands back a `Stream.Readable Bytes` rather than the bytes,
because a range can be any size and a stream is how Node says so. The pieces
come back in whatever sizes the operating system felt like; `Bytes.flatten`
puts them together.
-}
read : FileSystem.Permission -> Path -> Range -> Cmd Msg
read permission path range =
    FileSystem.readFileStream permission (FileSystem.Between { start = range.start, end = range.end - 1 }) path
        |> Task.mapError FileSystem.errorToString
        |> Task.andThen
            (\stream ->
                Stream.readUntilClosed (\part sofar -> Ok (Builder.pushLast part sofar)) (Builder.empty 4) stream
                    |> Task.mapError Stream.errorToString
            )
        |> Task.map (\parts -> Bytes.flatten (Builder.toArray parts))
        |> Task.attempt
            (\result ->
                when result is
                    Ok bytes ->
                        ReadBack { range = range, bytes = bytes }

                    Err reason ->
                        Failed reason
            )


{-| `Bytes` cannot be indexed, so a chunk is decoded into numbers once rather
than a byte at a time on every render.

`Array.Builder` and not `Array.pushLast`: `pushLast` is `toSpliced`, a copy of
the whole array, so building sixteen thousand of them one at a time is sixteen
thousand copies -- a hundred and thirty million element writes for one scroll.
The builder is the reason a chunk can be this size.
-}
toByteArray : Bytes -> Array Int
toByteArray bites =
    Decode.decode (byteArray (Bytes.length bites)) bites
        |> Maybe.withDefault []


byteArray : Int -> Decode.Decoder (Array Int)
byteArray count =
    Decode.loop
        { left = count, sofar = Builder.empty count }
        (\state ->
            if state.left <= 0 then
                Decode.succeed (Decode.Done (Builder.toArray state.sofar))

            else
                Decode.map
                    (\byte ->
                        Decode.Loop
                            { left = state.left - 1
                            , sofar = Builder.pushLast byte state.sofar
                            }
                    )
                    Decode.unsignedInt8
        )


byteAt : Model -> Int -> Maybe Int
byteAt model offset =
    at (offset - model.chunk.start) model.chunk.bytes


-- OPENING


{-| Where a browse starts: the directory already being browsed, else the one
the open file is in, else wherever the program was run.
-}
startingPoint : Model -> Task FileSystem.Error Path
startingPoint model =
    when model.browsing is
        Just browsing ->
            Task.succeed browsing.path

        Nothing ->
            when model.open is
                Just source ->
                    Task.succeed
                        (when source.from is
                            OnDisk path ->
                                Maybe.withDefault (Path.fromPosixString ".") (Path.parentPath path)

                            Pasted ->
                                Path.fromPosixString "."
                        )

                Nothing ->
                    FileSystem.currentWorkingDirectory model.permission


browseFrom : Model -> Cmd Msg
browseFrom model =
    listing model (startingPoint model)


browse : Model -> Path -> Cmd Msg
browse model path =
    listing model (Task.succeed path)


{-| `TFileDialog` reads the directory from inside itself; nothing here can,
because listing one is a `Task`. So opening a file is two steps -- read, then
show -- and walking into a directory is those same two steps again, which is
why `Statted` can answer a directory by starting over.
-}
listing : Model -> Task FileSystem.Error Path -> Cmd Msg
listing model start =
    start
        |> Task.andThen
            (\path ->
                FileSystem.listDirectory model.permission path
                    |> Task.map (\entries -> { path = path, entries = entries })
            )
        |> Task.attempt
            (\result ->
                when result is
                    Ok found ->
                        Listed found

                    Err error ->
                        Failed (FileSystem.errorToString error)
            )


statFile : Model -> Path -> Cmd Msg
statFile model path =
    FileSystem.metadata model.permission { resolveLink = True } path
        |> Task.attempt
            (\result ->
                when result is
                    Ok meta ->
                        Statted { path = path, meta = meta }

                    Err error ->
                        Failed (FileSystem.errorToString error)
            )


{-| Directories first and each marked with a slash, because the list is the
only place the dialog can say which rows go somewhere and which open something.
-}
entriesOf :
    { path : Path, entries : Array { path : Path, entityType : FileSystem.EntityType } }
    -> Array Entry
entriesOf listed =
    let
        named =
            listed.entries
                |> Array.map
                    (\entry ->
                        { name = Path.filenameWithExtension entry.path
                        , path = Path.append entry.path listed.path
                        , isDir = entry.entityType == FileSystem.Directory
                        }
                    )
                |> Array.sortWith
                    (\a b ->
                        if a.isDir == b.isDir then
                            compare a.name b.name

                        else if a.isDir then
                            LT

                        else
                            GT
                    )
                |> Array.map
                    (\entry ->
                        if entry.isDir then
                            { entry | name = entry.name ++ "/" }

                        else
                            entry
                    )
    in
    when Path.parentPath listed.path is
        Just up ->
            Array.pushFirst { name = "../", path = up, isDir = True } named

        Nothing ->
            named


fileDialog : Model -> { path : Path, entries : Array Entry } -> Tui.DialogSpec
fileDialog model browsing =
    Tui.fileDialog
        { id = fileDialogId
        , title = "Open file"
        , path = Path.toPosixString browsing.path
        , name = ""
        , entries = Array.map .name browsing.entries
        , history = model.recent
        , buttons = Tui.okCancelButtons
        , desktop = model.desktop
        }


answerFile : { id : String, cmd : String, values : Json.Decode.Value } -> Model -> Step
answerFile closed model =
    when model.browsing is
        Nothing ->
            still model

        Just browsing ->
            if closed.cmd /= "ok" then
                still { model | browsing = Nothing }

            else
                let
                    typed =
                        Tui.text "fileName" closed.values

                    chosen =
                        if typed /= "" then
                            -- The field wins when there is anything in it,
                            -- which is what makes the history drop-down mean
                            -- anything: what it puts back is a path, and a
                            -- path is not a row of the list beside it.
                            Path.fromPosixString typed

                        else
                            when at (Tui.number "fileList" closed.values) browsing.entries is
                                Just entry ->
                                    entry.path

                                Nothing ->
                                    browsing.path
                in
                { model = { model | message = "" }, command = statFile model chosen }


{-| `Array.get` counts from the end when the index is negative, so an index
that came from anywhere else is checked before it is used.
-}
at : Int -> Array a -> Maybe a
at index array =
    if index < 0 then
        Nothing

    else
        Array.get index array


remember : String -> Array String -> Array String
remember path history =
    Array.pushFirst path (Array.keepIf (\old -> old /= path) history)
        |> Array.takeFirst 8


-- GOING TO AN OFFSET


{-| Turbo Vision has no "ask for one number" dialog and neither does this
package: `messageBox` and `fileDialog` are helpers over `DialogSpec`, and a
`DialogSpec` is a window's worth of ordinary views. So this is one, written
out. It is a dozen lines of layout and it is the whole mechanism.

The field takes hex digits and an `x` as well as decimal, and `allowed` is what
says so -- a `TFilterValidator`, so a rejected keystroke never happens rather
than being complained about afterwards.
-}
gotoDialog : Model -> Tui.DialogSpec
gotoDialog model =
    let
        width =
            clamp 24 model.desktop.cols 46

        height =
            clamp 8 model.desktop.rows 10

        left =
            max 0 ((model.desktop.cols - width) // 2)

        top =
            max 0 ((model.desktop.rows - height) // 2)
    in
    { id = gotoDialogId
    , title = "Go to offset"
    , rect = { x1 = left, y1 = top, x2 = left + width, y2 = top + height }
    , views =
        -- The field before the label, and not for looks: a `Label` names a
        -- view that has to exist already, so listing them the other way round
        -- throws out of the builder and takes the dialog with it.
        [ InputLine
            { id = gotoFieldId
            , rect = { x1 = 11, y1 = 2, x2 = width - 3, y2 = 3 }
            , maxLen = 20
            , value = ""
            , allowed = Just "0123456789abcdefABCDEFxX"
            }
        , Label
            { id = "hex.goto.label"
            , rect = { x1 = 3, y1 = 2, x2 = 11, y2 = 3 }
            , text = "O~f~fset"
            , for = gotoFieldId
            }
        , StaticText
            { id = "hex.goto.hint"
            , rect = { x1 = 3, y1 = 4, x2 = width - 3, y2 = 5 }
            , text = "decimal, or 0x1F00 for hex"
            }
        , Button
            { id = "hex.goto.ok"
            , rect = { x1 = width - 28, y1 = height - 4, x2 = width - 16, y2 = height - 2 }
            , title = "~O~K"
            , cmd = "ok"
            , isDefault = True
            , takesFocus = True
            }
        , Button
            { id = "hex.goto.cancel"
            , rect = { x1 = width - 15, y1 = height - 4, x2 = width - 3, y2 = height - 2 }
            , title = "Cancel"
            , cmd = "cancel"
            , isDefault = False
            , takesFocus = True
            }
        ]
    }


answerGoto : { id : String, cmd : String, values : Json.Decode.Value } -> Model -> Step
answerGoto closed model =
    if closed.cmd /= "ok" then
        still model

    else
        let
            typed =
                Tui.text gotoFieldId closed.values
        in
        when parseOffset typed is
            Just offset ->
                goTo offset { model | message = "" }

            Nothing ->
                still { model | message = "Not an offset: " ++ typed }


parseOffset : String -> Maybe Int
parseOffset raw =
    let
        text =
            String.trim raw
    in
    if String.startsWith "0x" (String.toLower text) then
        fromHex (String.dropFirst 2 text)

    else
        String.toInt text


fromHex : String -> Maybe Int
fromHex text =
    if String.isEmpty text then
        Nothing

    else
        Array.foldl
            (\char sofar ->
                when sofar is
                    Nothing ->
                        Nothing

                    Just total ->
                        when String.firstIndexOf (String.toUpper (String.fromChar char)) "0123456789ABCDEF" is
                            Just digit ->
                                Just (total * 16 + digit)

                            Nothing ->
                                Nothing
            )
            (Just 0)
            (String.toArray text)


-- MENU


menu : MenuItem
menu =
    SubMenu
        { title = "~B~ytes"
        , key = ""
        , items =
            -- Three ways to get something to look at, then the ways of
            -- moving around it, then the ways of taking part of it with you.
            [ Item { title = "~O~pen file...", cmd = "hex.open", key = "", shortcut = "" }
            , Item { title = "~P~aste as text", cmd = "hex.pasteText", key = "", shortcut = "p" }
            , Item { title = "Paste he~x~ bytes", cmd = "hex.pasteHex", key = "", shortcut = "P" }
            , Separator
            , Item { title = "~G~o to offset...", cmd = "hex.goto", key = "", shortcut = "" }
            , Item { title = "~T~op of file", cmd = "hex.top", key = "", shortcut = "Ctrl-Home" }
            , Item { title = "~E~nd of file", cmd = "hex.end", key = "", shortcut = "Ctrl-End" }
            , Separator
            -- What is marked, or the highlight under the cursor. Both entries
            -- name their key as far as the eye is concerned, and neither binds
            -- one: the canvas has focus and the key goes there.
            , Item { title = "Cop~y~ bytes", cmd = "hex.copyHex", key = "", shortcut = "y" }
            , Item { title = "Copy as a ~d~ump", cmd = "hex.copyDump", key = "", shortcut = "" }
            , Separator
            , SubMenu
                { title = "~H~ighlight"
                , key = ""
                , items = highlightMenu
                }
            ]
        }


{-| Every entry here names a key and binds none, and that is the same rule the
calculator's keypad follows from the other side. A `key` on a menu item is an
Alt- accelerator, and the canvas has focus and eats plain letters, so binding
`v` here would either do nothing or take the letter away from the thing it is
for. The `shortcut` column is text: it says what to press, and pressing it goes
to the canvas, where [`pressKey`](#pressKey) is what actually answers.

So the menu is how somebody *finds* the mode, and the keys are how somebody
uses it. Neither is a second implementation of the other -- both ends run the
same four functions.
-}
highlightMenu : Array MenuItem
highlightMenu =
    [ Item { title = "~M~ark from here", cmd = "hex.mark", key = "", shortcut = "v" }
    , Item { title = "Mark whole ~r~ows", cmd = "hex.markRows", key = "", shortcut = "V" }
    , Separator
    ]
        ++ Array.indexedMap inkItem Theme.highlightNames
        ++ [ Separator
           , Item { title = "R~e~move under cursor", cmd = "hex.unpaint", key = "", shortcut = "d" }
           , Item { title = "~C~lear all", cmd = "hex.clear", key = "", shortcut = "" }
           ]


inkItem : Int -> String -> MenuItem
inkItem index name =
    Item
        { title = "~" ++ String.fromInt (index + 1) ++ "~  " ++ name
        , cmd = inkCommand ++ String.fromInt (index + 1)
        , key = ""
        , shortcut = String.fromInt (index + 1)
        }


inkName : Int -> String
inkName index =
    when Array.get index Theme.highlightNames is
        Just name ->
            name

        Nothing ->
            String.fromInt (index + 1)


-- VIEW


view : Inks -> Model -> Tui.Window
view inks model =
    { id = windowId
    , title = title model
    -- The widest window in the program, and it has to be: a dump row is
    -- seventy-six columns and cannot be narrower. Every rectangle below is
    -- written for *this* height and never recomputed, even after the user has
    -- dragged the window taller. Two reasons, and they are both about what a
    -- rectangle in a render means:
    --
    -- A view's rectangle is structural -- change one and the window is torn
    -- down and built again, losing the caret and the z-order -- so a layout
    -- that recomputed itself on every resize would rebuild the window on every
    -- resize. The `Grows` wrappers do the same arithmetic in `TGroup::
    -- changeBounds` instead, where it costs nothing.
    --
    -- And the *window's* rectangle is what the differ compares the next render
    -- against. Leaving it alone is what leaves the window the size the user
    -- dragged it to; rendering the height back would be a `setBounds` writing
    -- the size that is already there.
    , rect = { x1 = 0, y1 = 0, x2 = 79, y2 = 2 + openRows + 4 }
    -- Taller shows more of the file. Wider shows the same rows with space
    -- beside them, because sixteen bytes to a row is the format and not a
    -- layout choice -- so the frame does not offer it.
    , resize = Tui.resizeHeight
    , palette = Tui.BlueWindow
    , views =
        [ Canvas
            { id = "hex.header"
            , rect = { x1 = 1, y1 = 1, x2 = 1 + lineWidth, y2 = 2 }
            , lines = [ header inks ]
            , cursor = Nothing
            , takesFocus = False
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = Tui.stretchHeight
            , view =
                Canvas
                    { id = canvasId
                    , rect = { x1 = 1, y1 = 2, x2 = 1 + lineWidth, y2 = 2 + openRows }
                    , lines = dump inks model
                    , cursor = cursorAt model
                    , takesFocus = True
                    }
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = Tui.stretchHeight
            , view =
                ScrollBar
                    { id = scrollId
                    -- Against the frame, with column 77 left empty: a scroll
                    -- bar touching the last column of the ASCII rendering
                    -- reads as a seventeenth character.
                    , rect = { x1 = 2 + lineWidth, y1 = 2, x2 = 3 + lineWidth, y2 = 2 + openRows }
                    , value = divideBy bytesPerRow model.top
                    , min = 0
                    , max =
                        when model.open is
                            Just source ->
                                divideBy bytesPerRow (maxTop model source)

                            Nothing ->
                                0
                    , pageStep = model.rows
                    , arrowStep = 1
                    -- The dump is the only thing in this window that scrolls,
                    -- so the wheel is taken from anywhere in it -- including
                    -- with the pointer over the dump itself, which is where a
                    -- hand already is.
                    , for = ""
                    }
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = Tui.pinBottom
            , view =
                StaticText
                    { id = "hex.where"
                    , rect = { x1 = 1, y1 = 3 + openRows, x2 = 1 + lineWidth, y2 = 4 + openRows }
                    , text = whereItIs model
                    }
            }
        , Grows
            { grow = Tui.pinBottom
            , view =
                Canvas
                    { id = "hex.status"
                    , rect = { x1 = 1, y1 = 4 + openRows, x2 = 1 + lineWidth, y2 = 5 + openRows }
                    , lines = [ status inks model ]
                    , cursor = Nothing
                    , takesFocus = False
                    }
            }
        ]
    }


title : Model -> String
title model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            "Hex Dump"

        Just source ->
            "Hex Dump - "
                ++ source.name
                ++ " ("
                ++ String.fromInt source.size
                ++ " bytes)"


header : Inks -> Array Span
header inks =
    [ Tui.plain (String.repeat hexStart " ")
    , Tui.ink inks.ruler (String.join " " (Array.map columnLabel (Array.range 0 7)))
    , Tui.plain "  "
    , Tui.ink inks.ruler (String.join " " (Array.map columnLabel (Array.range 8 15)))
    , Tui.plain "  "
    , Tui.ink inks.ruler (String.join "" (Array.map Ascii.hexDigit (Array.range 0 15)))
    ]


columnLabel : Int -> String
columnLabel column =
    "0" ++ Ascii.hexDigit column


{-| Sixteen rows, whatever the file is. A dump that grew and shrank as it ran
off the end would move the two lines under it every time somebody pressed Down,
and there is nothing past the last row worth reading anyway.
-}
dump : Inks -> Model -> Array (Array Span)
dump inks model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            Array.map
                (\index ->
                    if index == 2 then
                        [ Tui.plain (String.repeat 14 " " ++ "Nothing open.  Bytes ▸ Open file, or p to paste.") ]

                    else
                        [ Tui.plain "" ]
                )
                (Array.range 0 (model.rows - 1))

        Just source ->
            Array.map (row inks model source) (Array.range 0 (model.rows - 1))


row : Inks -> Model -> Source -> Int -> Array Span
row inks model source index =
    let
        base =
            model.top + index * bytesPerRow
    in
    if base >= source.size then
        [ Tui.plain "" ]

    else
        [ Tui.ink inks.ruler (offsetText base), Tui.plain "  " ]
            ++ hexCells inks model source base
            ++ [ Tui.plain "  " ]
            ++ asciiCells inks model source base


{-| Eight bytes, a wider gap, eight bytes. The gap is what makes a row
countable without counting: everything past it is at offset eight or more.
-}
hexCells : Inks -> Model -> Source -> Int -> Array Span
hexCells inks model source base =
    Array.mapAndFlatten
        (\column ->
            (if column == 0 then
                []

             else if column == 8 then
                [ Tui.plain "  " ]

             else
                [ Tui.plain " " ]
            )
                ++ [ paint inks model (base + column) (pairText model source (base + column)) ]
        )
        (Array.range 0 (bytesPerRow - 1))


asciiCells : Inks -> Model -> Source -> Int -> Array Span
asciiCells inks model source base =
    Array.map
        (\column ->
            let
                offset =
                    base + column
            in
            if offset >= source.size then
                Tui.plain " "

            else
                when byteAt model offset is
                    Just code ->
                        when face inks model offset is
                            -- A byte wearing something wears it whether or not
                            -- it is printable: `dim` is what a byte nobody has
                            -- said anything about looks like, and a
                            -- highlighted one has been.
                            Just pair ->
                                Tui.on pair.bg (Tui.ink pair.fg (Ascii.glyph code))

                            Nothing ->
                                if code >= 32 && code < 127 then
                                    Tui.plain (Ascii.glyph code)

                                else
                                    Tui.ink inks.dim (Ascii.glyph code)

                    Nothing ->
                        Tui.ink inks.dim " "
        )
        (Array.range 0 (bytesPerRow - 1))


{-| The byte in hex, or what to draw where a byte is not: past the end of the
file, nothing at all; inside it but not yet read, two dots. The second lasts
for one frame, and it is the only place in the program that admits the file is
not in memory.
-}
pairText : Model -> Source -> Int -> String
pairText model source offset =
    if offset >= source.size then
        "  "

    else
        when byteAt model offset is
            Just code ->
                String.padLeft 2 '0' (Ascii.radix 16 code)

            Nothing ->
                ".."


{-| The selected byte is painted rather than merely pointed at, for the reason
the ASCII chart's is: whether a terminal shows its cursor at all is the
terminal's business. The hardware cursor is kept in step underneath, for the
ones that do and for the tests.
-}
paint : Inks -> Model -> Int -> String -> Span
paint inks model offset text =
    when face inks model offset is
        Just pair ->
            Tui.on pair.bg (Tui.ink pair.fg text)

        Nothing ->
            Tui.plain text


{-| What a byte is wearing, and the order the three of them win in. All three
can be on the screen at once -- a mark being dragged across an old highlight,
with the cursor somewhere inside it -- and the order is forced by what each one
is *for*:

The cursor is on top because it is the answer to "where am I", and a mode that
hid it would be a mode you could get lost in. The mark is next because it is
the thing being decided; the highlights are what has already been decided, and
a decision in progress is allowed to cover one that is finished. Underneath,
nothing: plain text, so that most of a dump is the colour of the window and the
paint means something.

Both columns read this, which is the point of it being a function rather than
two similar `if`s -- the hex pair and its printable character being the same
colour is the only thing that says they are the same byte.
-}
face : Inks -> Model -> Int -> Maybe { fg : Hue, bg : Hue }
face inks model offset =
    if offset == model.cursor then
        Just inks.selected

    else if within (markedRange model) offset then
        Just inks.marking

    else
        inkAt model offset
            |> Maybe.andThen (\index -> Array.get index inks.highlights)


offsetText : Int -> String
offsetText offset =
    String.padLeft 8 '0' (Ascii.radix 16 offset)


hexColumn : Int -> Int
hexColumn column =
    hexStart
        + (column * 3)
        + (if column >= 8 then
            1

           else
            0
          )


cursorAt : Model -> Maybe { x : Int, y : Int }
cursorAt model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            Nothing

        Just _ ->
            let
                line =
                    divideBy bytesPerRow (model.cursor - model.top)
            in
            if line >= 0 && line < model.rows then
                Just
                    { x = hexColumn (Math.modBy bytesPerRow model.cursor)
                    , y = line
                    }

            else
                Nothing


whereItIs : Model -> String
whereItIs model =
    when model.open is
        Nothing ->
            ""

        Just _ ->
            "Offset "
                ++ offsetText model.cursor
                ++ String.padRight 14 ' ' (" (" ++ String.fromInt model.cursor ++ ")")
                ++ (when byteAt model model.cursor is
                        Just code ->
                            Ascii.bases code

                        Nothing ->
                            ""
                   )


{-| The message line, and the byte's name when there is no message.

A message is not cleared by the next keystroke. The calculator taught that one:
`update` was wiping its hint on every key, so the only line explaining the tool
was gone before anybody could read it. This one goes when the next thing the
user asks for succeeds.
-}
status : Inks -> Model -> Array Span
status inks model =
    if model.message /= "" then
        [ Tui.ink inks.alert model.message ]

    else
        when model.marking is
            -- A mode with no sign of itself on the screen is a program that
            -- has stopped answering, so the line the byte's name lives on says
            -- what is marked and how to get out of it instead. It is also the
            -- second half of the lesson the calculator taught: the hint is
            -- here for as long as the mode is, rather than shown once and
            -- wiped by the next keystroke.
            Just marking ->
                when markedRange model is
                    Just range ->
                        markLine inks marking range

                    Nothing ->
                        [ Tui.plain "" ]

            Nothing ->
                when model.open is
                    Nothing ->
                        [ Tui.plain "" ]

                    Just _ ->
                        when byteAt model model.cursor is
                            Just code ->
                                [ Tui.plain (Ascii.naming code) ] ++ inkNote inks model

                            Nothing ->
                                [ Tui.plain "" ]


markLine : Inks -> Marking -> Range -> Array Span
markLine inks marking range =
    [ Tui.on inks.marking.bg
        (Tui.ink inks.marking.fg
            (when marking.grain is
                ByByte ->
                    " MARK "

                ByRow ->
                    " MARK ROWS "
            )
        )
    , Tui.plain
        ("  "
            ++ offsetText range.start
            ++ "-"
            ++ offsetText (max range.start (range.end - 1))
            ++ "  "
            ++ String.fromInt (range.end - range.start)
            ++ (if range.end - range.start == 1 then
                    " byte "

                else
                    " bytes "
               )
        )
    ]
        ++ swatches inks
        ++ [ Tui.ink inks.dim "  Esc cancel" ]


{-| The six keys, each wearing what it paints.

It said `1-6 paint` before this, which is a range of numbers and not an answer
to the question somebody in the mode is actually asking -- *which one is
yellow*. A key cap is the one place a colour can be named by being it, and it
costs eighteen columns on a line that had them.

The order is the array's, so a scheme that reordered its grounds would reorder
the legend without being asked to, and the number on the cap is the number the
key sends -- both because there is only one place, [`inkOf`](#inkOf), that
knows the six are numbered from one.
-}
swatches : Inks -> Array Span
swatches inks =
    Array.indexedMap
        (\index pair ->
            Tui.on pair.bg (Tui.ink pair.fg (" " ++ String.fromInt (index + 1) ++ " "))
        )
        inks.highlights


{-| The swatch is the colour itself rather than its name in the theme's words,
because the name is what the menu says and the colour is what the screen says,
and the byte under the cursor is the one place both are available at once.
-}
inkNote : Inks -> Model -> Array Span
inkNote inks model =
    when inkAt model model.cursor is
        Nothing ->
            []

        Just index ->
            [ Tui.plain "  "
            , when Array.get index inks.highlights is
                Just pair ->
                    -- The number as well as the name, because this is the
                    -- other place the two are both available and the legend
                    -- above is the one somebody has to read them off.
                    Tui.on pair.bg
                        (Tui.ink pair.fg
                            (" " ++ String.fromInt (index + 1) ++ " " ++ inkName index ++ " ")
                        )

                Nothing ->
                    Tui.plain ""
            , Tui.ink inks.dim "  d removes it"
            ]
